# ============================================================================
# PROBE ONLY -- BUILDS NO MESH, EXPORTS NOTHING, OVERWRITES NOTHING.
# ============================================================================
# Purpose: print the IDs of the two "major-axis" curves on the fracture plane
# for each Kalantar2025 specimen, so they can be pasted into the three
# production journals, which need them to put a node exactly at each borehole.
#
# WHY THOSE TWO CURVES MATTER
# ---------------------------
# Both boreholes sit at y = 0 on the fracture plane. That is not the interior of
# a surface -- it is exactly the line where the `webcut ... yplane` cut the
# fracture ellipse, i.e. the ellipse's MAJOR AXIS, and it is a geometric curve.
# Cubit divides that curve into N equal intervals, so the only node positions
# available to the source are k/N along it. The design borehole sits at
# x/r = (24.99 - 5)/24.99 = 0.799920, so the pinning error is integer
# arithmetic: round(0.79992 N)/N - 0.79992.
#
# Measured on the six meshes already built (scripts/check_axis_intervals.py):
#
#     mesh              N    nearest fraction     pinning error
#     _size3  OG-SH     25   20/25 = 0.800000         4.1 um   <-- N divisible by 5
#     _size3  OG-T      27   22/27 = 0.814815       792.9 um
#     _size3  OG-SC     26   21/26 = 0.807692       388.5 um
#     graded  OG-SH     28   22/28 = 0.785714       732.2 um
#     graded  OG-T      29   23/29 = 0.793103       362.8 um
#     graded  OG-SC     27   22/27 = 0.814815       744.4 um
#
# All six reproduce the independently measured check_source_nodes.py distance to
# 0.1 um. OG-SH pinned at 4.1 um because 25 divides by 5, not because its mesh
# was better.
#
# HOW TO USE
# ----------
#   1. Run this journal in Cubit. It builds each geometry, stops before meshing,
#      and lists the curves lying in the four fracture surfaces.
#   2. In each listing find the TWO curves whose length equals the half-major-axis
#      quoted below. That length is unique in the model -- the minor-axis halves
#      are 24.99 mm, the sidewall arcs are elliptical quarters, and the axial
#      edges are the specimen length -- so there is no ambiguity.
#   3. Paste the two IDs into the matching production journal, at the lines
#      marked  c_pos / c_neg .
#
#      c_pos is the one running to POSITIVE x (towards z_high),
#      c_neg the one running to NEGATIVE x (towards z_low).
#      `list curve <id>` prints its end vertices, which is how to tell them apart.
#
# Curve IDs are deterministic for a given construction order, exactly like the
# surface and vertex IDs already hardcoded in the production journals. They only
# need to be recovered once, and only re-checked if the construction order above
# `imprint all` ever changes.
# ============================================================================


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- OG-SH, 29 deg
# half-major-axis = r / sin(29) = 51.5460 mm
reset
create Cylinder height 0.12000 radius 0.02499
move Volume 1  z 0.060 include_merged
create vertex location 0.02499 0.02499 0.105083153
create vertex location 0.02499 -0.02499 0.105083153
create vertex location -0.02499 -0.02499 0.014916847
webcut volume 1 with plane vertex 3 vertex 4 vertex 5
delete vertex 3 4 5
webcut volume all with plane xplane imprint merge
webcut volume all with plane yplane imprint merge
imprint all
merge all
echo "=== OG-SH: look for the two curves of length 0.0515460 m ==="
list curve in surface 26 46 53 32


# ----------------------------------------------------------------- OG-T, 28 deg
# half-major-axis = r / sin(28) = 53.2301 mm
reset
create Cylinder height 0.10000 radius 0.02499
move Volume 1  z 0.050 include_merged
create vertex location 0.02499 0.02499 0.096999354
create vertex location 0.02499 -0.02499 0.096999354
create vertex location -0.02499 -0.02499 0.003000646
webcut volume 1 with plane vertex 3 vertex 4 vertex 5
delete vertex 3 4 5
webcut volume all with plane xplane imprint merge
webcut volume all with plane yplane imprint merge
imprint all
merge all
echo "=== OG-T: look for the two curves of length 0.0532301 m ==="
list curve in surface 26 46 53 32


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- OG-SC, 30 deg
# half-major-axis = r / sin(30) = 49.9800 mm
reset
create Cylinder height 0.10000 radius 0.02499
move Volume 1  z 0.050 include_merged
create vertex location 0.02499 0.02499 0.093283950
create vertex location 0.02499 -0.02499 0.093283950
create vertex location -0.02499 -0.02499 0.006716050
webcut volume 1 with plane vertex 3 vertex 4 vertex 5
delete vertex 3 4 5
webcut volume all with plane xplane imprint merge
webcut volume all with plane yplane imprint merge
imprint all
merge all
echo "=== OG-SC: look for the two curves of length 0.0499800 m ==="
list curve in surface 26 46 53 32
